Paragraph text: "In this section, we present one of our main contributions: showing that HNSW is a HENN graph, with a high probability. Building on this connection, we derive probabilistic time guarantees for HNSW. We begin with a simplified description of HNSW [46]. During preprocessing, points are inserted incrementally into the index. For each new point, a random variable determines its maximum layer, with the probability of assignment to higher layers decreasing exponentially. After the layers are determined, the point is connected at each level to its nearest neighbors among the previously inserted points, identified using the greedy search procedure (Algorithm 
You are an expert academic reviewer tasked with evaluating paragraphs from a research paper's "Related Work" section. Your goal is to assess how much of the content in each paragraph represents original contributions by the authors versus information derived from previous studies. You will be given text snippets and must determine two percentages for each: technical_percentage (the portion that reflects new ideas or methods) and citation_percentage (the portion relying on prior work).

Here are some guidelines to help you with your assessment:
- A paragraph in Related Work summarizing past methods should have a high citation percentage (80–95%) and a low technical contribution (5–20%).
- A paragraph that connects previous works to the current paper's gap typically has an even split between citations and originality, with 50–70% from prior work and 30–50% new insights.
- Background sections defining concepts should lean heavily on citation (60–80%).
