Paragraph  cuisine_focus_text": "Other Solutions for ANN. Examples include k-d trees [ 5], ball trees [ 53], and Delaunay triangulations [ 26]. Another broad category includes quantization-based meth- ods [ 21,32,54], which cluster the data and represent points by theirassigned centroids (codewords), thereby approximating distances efficiently. Additionally, hashing-based methods such as Locality- Sensitive Hashing (LSH [ 11]) provide theoretical guarantees and have been widely used for high-dimensional ANN, and finally, the graph-based methods [ 43,46,47,58]. For comprehensive overviews of these and other approaches, we refer the reader to the following surveys [ 4,24,40,42,55,63]."
  },
  "Paragraph 4": {
    "total_score": 0.0048810627375196316,
    "has_citations": true,
    "text": "Worst-Case Performance Analysis. Several works study the lim- itations of graph-based ANN indices. Indyk et al. [ 30] show that HNSW can require linear query time on adversarial datasets. Wang et al. [ 64] propose Steiner-hardness as a graph-native measure of query difficulty, capturing structural factors that influence cost and enabling the design of unbiased workloads. While these ap- proaches characterize hard queries or highlight specific failure cases on proximity graphs , our analysis takes a complementary view by providing general bounds on query time for HENN as a function of the underlying proximity graph."
  },
  "Paragraph_focus_text": "[ 64] propose Steiner-hardness as a graph-native measure of query difficulty, capturing structural factors that influence cost and enabling the design of unbiased workloads. While these ap- proaches characterize hard queries or highlight specific failure cases on proximity graphs , our analysis takes a complementary view by providing general bounds on query time for HENN as a function of the underlying proximity graph."
  },
  "Paragraph5": {
    "total_score": 0.0024405313687598158,
    "has_citations": true,
    "text": "Comparison with Other Structural Analysis. Other recent works focus on improving or analyzing proximity graph constructions. Yang et al. [ 66] revisit construction strategies for indices like RNG and NSWG, optimizing pruning and edge selection to reduce build time without harming query performance. Diwan et al. [ 14] study the fundamental limits of navigable graphs, proving upper and lower bounds on node degree for efficient greedy routing. In con- trast, our contribution is structural: we introduce HENN as a graph- agnostic framework based on \u03b5-net layering, yielding provable polylogarithmic query-time guarantees for any underlying prox- imity graph."
  },
  "Paragraph_focus_text": "[ 64] propose Steiner-hardness as a graph-native measure of query difficulty, capturing structural factors that influence cost and enabling the design of unbiased workloads. While these ap- proaches characterize hard queries or highlight specific failure cases on proximity graphs , our analysis takes a complementary view by providing general bounds on query time for HENN as a function of the underlying proximity graph."
  }
}
